      },
      "hints": [
        "The tangent and normal gradients have product $-1$, so a normal gradient of $-2$ requires a tangent gradient of $\\frac12$.",
        "Use $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt t}$ from part (a) and solve $\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt t}=\\dfrac12$ for $t>0$.",
        "Substitute that parameter value into both $x=\\sqrt t+3$ and $y=\\ln t$, keeping the coordinates exact."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Use the perpendicular relation between the gradient of the normal $m_N$ and the gradient of the tangent $m_T$:\n$$m_T = -\\dfrac{1}{m_N} = -\\dfrac{1}{-2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$$",
        "Set $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ using the result from part (a):\n$$\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{t}} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$$\n$$\\sqrt{t} = 4$$\n$$t = 16$$",
        "Calculate the exact coordinates at $t = 16$:\n$$x = \\sqrt{16} + 3 = 4 + 3 = 7$$\n$$y = \\ln(16) = \\ln(2^4) = 4\\ln 2$$",
        "State the exact coordinates of the point:\n$$(7, \\ln 16) \\quad (\\text{or } (7, 4\\ln 2))$$"
      ],
